            <ArticleTitle>Cervicogenic headache: diagnostic criteria.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>725-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Criteria for the diagnosis of cervicogenic headache are proposed, which include unilateral head pain, symptoms and signs of neck involvement, non-clustering episodic moderate pain originating in the neck then spreading to the head, and response to root or nerve blockade; plus rarer and non-obligatory features such as autonomic disturbances, dizziness, phonophotophobia, monocular visual blurring, and difficulty swallowing.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
